# ── Speech-to-text ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STT_PROVIDER: whisper_local -> open-source Whisper on THIS machine (default).
#                               Audio never leaves the server; no key, no quota.
#                               Needs `pip install openai-whisper` + ffmpeg on
#                               PATH; first use downloads the model file to
#                               ~/.cache/whisper (medium ≈ 1.4 GB).
#               gemini        -> Google audio understanding via GEMINI_API_KEY
STT_PROVIDER=whisper_local
# tiny | base | small | medium | large — bigger = more accurate but slower
# 2026-08-20: medium -> large, on measured evidence. Meeting transcription is a
# background job with nobody waiting on it, so latency does not matter here —
# accuracy on the languages our clients actually speak does. Tested with real
# neural Hindi/Kannada TTS (edge-tts), similarity to what was actually spoken:
#     Kannada  tiny 6-12% · small 15-18% · medium 8-67% · LARGE 88-94%
#     Hindi    tiny 8-12% · small 87-96% · medium 65-98%
# medium does not merely mishear Kannada — it transcribed two of three clips
# into SINHALA script, i.e. the wrong language entirely. large detects lang=kn
# correctly at 0.93-0.96 confidence. It is also FASTER on Kannada than small or
# medium (8.9s vs 15.5s/25.5s on a 2.4s clip), because Whisper retries with
# rising temperature when its own thresholds fail: a model that cannot
# understand the audio also burns extra decode passes.
# Needs ~10 GB VRAM — comfortable on the 12 GB office VM, tight on an 8 GB
# laptop. The embedding model is deliberately on CPU so it does not compete.
WHISPER_MODEL=large
# The model POST /api/v1/dictate uses for speaking into a text field in the
# app. Same ladder, deliberately smaller: a person is waiting on this one, so
# it is tuned for latency, not for a 40-minute recording. tiny ≈ 75 MB and
# transcribes a short clip in well under a second on CPU. If your team's names
# and jargon come back mangled, step up to base or small — the cost is a
# slower mic, not a broken one.
# Stays `tiny` deliberately, and it is a real trade, not an oversight. A person
# is watching a spinner here, so latency IS the product: tiny answers English in
# 0.1s where large takes 2.8s. English and code-switched Hinglish ("मेरे open
# tasks दिखाओ") both transcribe fine at tiny, because the load-bearing words are
# English. Hindi and Kannada SPOKEN INTO THE MIC do not (8-12% similarity) —
# those users should type, or this becomes `large`. Note `small` is the worst of
# both worlds: 15.5s AND still fails Kannada.
DICTATION_WHISPER_MODEL=large-v3

# ── Memory embeddings (semantic note search) ─────────────────────────────────
# 2026-08-20: moved OFF Google's paid embedding API and onto a local model.
# Why: gemini-embedding-001 returned 429 "prepayment credits are depleted", and
# the old code only fell back to the offline embedder when the key was ABSENT —
# a key that was present but broken raised instead, so add_memory logged a
# warning and stored nothing. Semantic search had been quietly dead since the
# credits ran out (memory_chunks 0 rows against client_intelligence 28).
#
# Model chosen on a measured bake-off over the real 31-document corpus, scored
# against 33 queries with known answers. Decisive axis was Indic scripts:
# all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (the model the dependencies memo proposed) tokenises Kannada
# entirely as [UNK], so six different Kannada questions produce vectors 0.94
# similar to one another and retrieval is 0/6. This model: Kannada 5/6,
# Devanagari 3/3, 26/33 overall, MRR 0.821.
#   local -> on this machine, no key/quota/cost  | gemini -> Google's API
#   hash  -> deterministic offline (no semantics; automatic when stub)
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=local
EMBEDDING_MODEL_LOCAL=intfloat/multilingual-e5-base
# Asymmetric model: stored text and search text take different prefixes and the
# pair must agree, or stored notes stop matching queries.
EMBEDDING_QUERY_PREFIX="query: "
EMBEDDING_PASSAGE_PREFIX="passage: "
# CPU on purpose — the GPU is Whisper's (~5 GB medium, ~10 GB large on a 12 GB
# card). Embedding is ~8 ms on CPU; competing for VRAM would risk an OOM in
# transcription for no gain.
EMBEDDING_DEVICE=cpu
# Used only when EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=gemini
EMBEDDING_MODEL=gemini-embedding-001

